---
/**
 * The caveats a figure genuinely needs, folded away.
 *
 * Both visualisations make claims that deserve qualifying, but five lines of
 * methodology under a picture get read by nobody and bury the picture. The
 * one-line caption stays visible; everything else is one click away and still
 * fully present for crawlers and screen readers, since <details> renders its
 * contents either way.
 */
---

<details class="note">
  <summary>how this is made</summary>
  <div class="body"><slot /></div>
</details>

<style>
  .note {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--fg-faint);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
  }

  summary {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 2rem;
    width: fit-content;
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--fg-faint);
  }

  /* `display: inline-flex` (needed for the tap target) suppresses the native
     marker, and a bare grey line does not read as something to click. */
  summary::before {
    content: '+';
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-inline-end: 0.5ch;
  }

  [open] > summary::before {
    content: '−';
  }

  summary:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .body {
    line-height: 1.55;
    padding-inline-start: 0.9rem;
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-block-end: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
